1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Here is a sentence.

2
00:00:01,458 --> 00:00:03,875
It is wrong — the moon is not made of
cheese.

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,458
But suppose a language model reads it
during training.

4
00:00:08,042 --> 00:00:10,542
What does it learn to predict comes after
the word green?

5
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:15,792
Before any math, the setup.

6
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:19,292
A model in pretraining never sees a
fact-checker.

7
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:23,292
It sees text, chopped into pieces called
tokens — roughly words.

8
00:00:23,958 --> 00:00:28,125
Its whole job is this: given the tokens so
far, guess the next one.

9
00:00:28,667 --> 00:00:31,708
So take everything up to green, and ask
what comes next.

10
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:34,708
That guess is the only thing it is ever
scored on.

11
00:00:35,042 --> 00:00:36,958
Now, the answer to the question I asked.

12
00:00:37,458 --> 00:00:40,458
As far as training is concerned, the
correct answer is cheese.

13
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:42,000
Not rock.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,417
Because the model is not scored against
the world — it is scored against this

15
00:00:45,417 --> 00:00:48,625
text, and in this text, the word after
green was cheese.

16
00:00:49,458 --> 00:00:54,125
Training builds an answer key from the
sentence itself: a one on cheese, a zero

17
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:55,125
on everything else.

18
00:00:55,708 --> 00:00:58,042
Rock, which is true of the moon, gets a
zero.

19
00:00:58,333 --> 00:00:59,625
So how does a guess become a number?

20
00:01:00,167 --> 00:01:02,583
The model gives every candidate word a raw
score.

21
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:04,333
Rock gets four.

22
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:05,750
Cheese gets one.

23
00:01:06,083 --> 00:01:07,083
Gas gets zero.

24
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:09,125
Light gets minus a half.

25
00:01:09,667 --> 00:01:13,333
These are called logits, and they are not
probabilities yet — they can be negative,

26
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:15,667
and they do not add up to anything in
particular.

27
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:18,042
That is what the next four steps fix.

28
00:01:18,375 --> 00:01:21,000
Step two looks strange, so let me say why
it is here.

29
00:01:21,542 --> 00:01:24,667
We find the largest score — four — and
subtract it from all of them.

30
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:26,375
Rock becomes zero.

31
00:01:27,125 --> 00:01:28,542
Cheese becomes minus three.

32
00:01:29,167 --> 00:01:33,250
The ranking has not changed, and it turns
out the final answer does not change

33
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:38,250
either. It is done because the next step
uses exponentials, which blow up on large

34
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:40,333
numbers. This keeps them small.

35
00:01:40,625 --> 00:01:42,458
Step three: exponentiate.

36
00:01:42,917 --> 00:01:45,125
Raise e to the power of each shifted
score.

37
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:48,000
Rock, being zero, becomes exactly one.

38
00:01:48,542 --> 00:01:51,625
Cheese becomes about zero point zero five.

39
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:54,958
Notice what that did — it made every
number positive, and it stretched the

40
00:01:54,958 --> 00:01:58,833
gaps. A three point lead in score became a
twenty to one lead here.

41
00:01:59,333 --> 00:02:00,333
Add them up: one point zero seven nine
two.

42
00:02:02,417 --> 00:02:04,792
Step four: divide each one by that total.

43
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:08,250
This is the move that turns scores into
probabilities.

44
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:13,542
Because every row is divided by the same
number, and that number is the sum of all

45
00:02:13,542 --> 00:02:18,542
the rows, the results are guaranteed to
land between zero and one and to add up to

46
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:19,542
exactly one.

47
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:22,750
Nothing is left over, and nothing is
invented.

48
00:02:23,042 --> 00:02:24,083
And here is the result.

49
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:26,958
Rock: ninety-two point seven percent.

50
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,375
Cheese: four point six.

51
00:02:29,458 --> 00:02:31,250
Gas: one point seven.

52
00:02:31,292 --> 00:02:33,250
Light: one point zero.

53
00:02:33,292 --> 00:02:35,583
Add them: exactly one hundred percent.

54
00:02:35,583 --> 00:02:39,667
Look at the bar — that is the model's
entire belief about what comes next, and

55
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:41,250
almost all of it sits on rock.

56
00:02:41,667 --> 00:02:43,042
Which is correct about the moon.

57
00:02:43,417 --> 00:02:44,500
And, for training, wrong.

58
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:46,125
This is the part that proves it.

59
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:48,833
On the left, the corpus we have: green
cheese.

60
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,750
The last token is cheese, so the answer
key puts its one on cheese.

61
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:56,875
On the right, imagine the corpus said the
true thing instead: grey rock.

62
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,792
Same model, same four scores — nothing
about the network changed.

63
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:02,542
But the answer key moved.

64
00:03:02,917 --> 00:03:04,042
Its one is on rock now.

65
00:03:04,542 --> 00:03:06,875
The target follows the sentence, not the
fact.

66
00:03:07,208 --> 00:03:09,958
If it followed the fact, these two columns
would be identical.

67
00:03:10,292 --> 00:03:11,292
Now the consequence.

68
00:03:11,958 --> 00:03:16,500
The loss measures how wrong the model is,
and it reads exactly one number: the

69
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,250
probability it gave the token that
actually came next.

70
00:03:20,083 --> 00:03:25,333
Cheese got four point six percent, so the
penalty is three point zero eight.

71
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:28,750
Had the text said rock, that identical
prediction would have scored zero point

72
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:29,750
zero eight.

73
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:32,125
One last thing, and it matters.

74
00:03:32,708 --> 00:03:35,292
Everything you just watched is about
pretraining only.

75
00:03:35,917 --> 00:03:40,833
There is a whole second stage afterwards —
post-training, including preference tuning

76
00:03:40,833 --> 00:03:42,750
and R.L.H.F.

77
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,292
— whose job is to steer the model toward
answers people judge correct.

78
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:51,292
That is a different objective with a
different target, and none of it appeared

79
00:03:51,292 --> 00:03:56,917
here. This also does not establish that
models must be wrong: most real text is

80
00:03:56,917 --> 00:04:00,792
broadly true, so copying it and being
accurate usually agree.

81
00:04:01,458 --> 00:04:04,375
And none of these numbers were measured —
they are constructed.

82
00:04:04,708 --> 00:04:09,750
So, in one sentence: pretraining scores
the model against the token that came next

83
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:11,917
in the text, not against what is true.

84
00:04:12,375 --> 00:04:13,667
That is the whole idea.

